{"url": "https://api.openalex.org/works?search=computational+frameworks+for+intrinsic+curiosity+and+interestingness+metrics+in+autonomous+scientific+discovery&per-page=4&select=id%2Cdoi%2Ctitle%2Cpublication_year%2Ctype%2Ccited_by_count%2Copen_access%2Cprimary_location%2Cabstract_inverted_index", "scope": "OpenAlex scholarly metadata and reconstructed abstracts where supplied; not full papers", "excerpt": "[{"id": "https://openalex.org/W7213482341", "doi": null, "title": "An Autonomous Platform for Reticular Materials Discovery", "publication_year": 2026, "type": "dissertation", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access": {"is_oa": true, "oa_status": "green", "oa_url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt2tk455m3/qt2tk455m3.pdf", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "pmh:oai:escholarship.org:ark:/13030/qt2tk455m3", "doi": null, "is_oa": true, "landing_page_url": "https://escholarship.org/uc/item/2tk455m3", "pdf_url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt2tk455m3/qt2tk455m3.pdf", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4306400115", "display_name": "eScholarship (California Digital Library)", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/I2801248553", "host_organization_name": "California Digital Library", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/I2801248553"], "host_organization_lineage_names": [], "type": "repository"}, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "submittedVersion", "is_accepted": false, "is_published": false, "raw_source_name": null, "raw_type": "etd"}, "abstract": "Reticular materials, including metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs), are porous crystalline structures constructed from molecular building blocks. Their structures can be rationally designed for a wide range of applications through control over building block composition, geometry, and functionality. However, the crystallization of reticular materials remains challenging. Strong chemical bonding between building units and the formation of competing phases result in a narrow crystallization window that requires precise control over reaction parameters. In the absence of a reliable theoretical framework for predicting crystallization outcomes, synthesis remains largely trial-and-error and dependent on human intuition. To address this limitation, this dissertation introduces an autonomous platform, termed Algorithmic Iterative Reticular Synthesis (AIRES), which integrates high-throughput experimentation and machine learning to identify correlations between successful crystallization outcomes, building block properties, and reaction parameters and therefore to accelerate the discovery of new reticular materials. Chapter 1 introduces the conceptual framework of autonomous platforms for chemistry and materials science, w

